@cindex Fresnel integrals

The Fresnel integrals are defined by @math{C(x) = \int_0^x \cos(\pi
t^2/2) dt} and @math{S(x) = \int_0^x \sin(\pi t^2/2) dt}.  Both
functions are odd, @math{C(-x) = -C(x)} and @math{S(-x) = -S(x)}.  The
routines are declared in the header file @file{gsl_sf_fresnel.h}.

@deftypefun double gsl_sf_fresnel_c (double @var{x})
@deftypefunx int gsl_sf_fresnel_c_e (double @var{x}, gsl_sf_result * @var{result})
These routines compute the Fresnel cosine integral @math{C(x)} for
@var{x}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun double gsl_sf_fresnel_s (double @var{x})
@deftypefunx int gsl_sf_fresnel_s_e (double @var{x}, gsl_sf_result * @var{result})
These routines compute the Fresnel sine integral @math{S(x)} for
@var{x}.
@end deftypefun

The values are computed in full double precision for moderate arguments.
For large @math{|x|} the phase @math{\pi x^2/2} can no longer be
represented exactly and the reported error grows with @math{|x|}.  An
infinite argument returns the error @code{GSL_EDOM}, since the integrals
have no limit there.
